# How do you learn from your own games?
teaches: ["training/game-review"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "How do you find the move where a game turned? Why keep a list of your own mistakes?"
next: {"title":"Week 1 review: habits under pressure","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/07-week-1-review-habits-under-pressure.cast"}

## Warm-up: two threats, then calculate
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 2r2rk1/pp2qppp/4p3/8/n7/2P5/PP3PPP/RB2QRK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qe4, f5, Qxa4]}
      - {fen: 5rk1/ppp2ppp/1qn5/8/2B5/5Q2/PPP2PPP/4R1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qxf7+, Rxf7, Re8#]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today: how do you learn from the games you lose? First, two warm-up puzzles from this week.
    draw position

    pause 0.8

B: Bishop takes h7, check! That wins a pawn.
    highlight piece_b1

A: A pawn, but there's better: one move that makes two threats.
    highlight piece_a4

    pause 0.8

    ask "Make two threats at once." widget chess line true hints ["One threat is mate on h7.","Your queen."] themes ["threat"] voice a teaches ["safety/threats"]
        right: Queen to e4 threatens mate on h7 and hits the loose knight on a4. Black can only stop one.
        wrong: Find a queen move that threatens mate and hits something loose.

B: He stops the mate, and the knight falls.
    point at.ref piece_a4 gesture circle

    pause 0.8

A: Second one: calculation. Checks first, then follow each one to the end.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Queen takes f7 is check. But the rook just takes my queen.
    highlight piece_f7

A: Then look again. What can that rook still do?
    highlight piece_f8

    pause 0.8

    ask "Calculate to the end." widget chess line true hints ["Start with the queen check.","After the recapture: your rook on e1."] themes ["combination","back rank"] voice a teaches ["calculation/combination","safety/back-rank"]
        right: Queen takes f7, rook takes, rook to e8: mate. The bishop on c4 pins the rook, so it cannot block.
        wrong: After the rook takes your queen, look at every check you have left.

B: Mate. The rook couldn't block: my bishop pins it.
    highlight piece_c4

A: Both wins sat one move past where you'd stop looking.
    highlight piece_f7

A: Today we look back the same way, at your own games.
    fade position to 0.35 duration 2

## Find where it turned
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4, Nf6, Nc3, Bc5, d3, d6, O-O, O-O, Bg5, h6, Bh4, g5, Nd5, gxh4, Nxf6+, Qxf6, c3, Bg4, Qb3, Na5, Qb4, Bxb4]}
      - {fen: r1bq1rk1/ppp2p2/2np1n1p/2bNp1p1/2B1P2B/3P1N2/PPP2PPP/R2Q1RK1 b - - 1 9, moves: [gxh4], flip: true}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
    arrows:
      - {from: g5, to: h4}
      - {from: h4, to: g3}
sources:
    - id: botvinnik
      title: 'Chess Tech University: philosophy of game analysis, part 2'
      authors: US Chess
      year: 2020
      finding: Botvinnik advised publishing your annotations so that others can criticise them; the engine now plays that role.
      url: https://new.uschess.org/news/chess-tech-university-philosophy-game-analysis-part-2/
    - id: botvinnik100
      title: One Hundred Selected Games
      authors: Mikhail Botvinnik
      year: 1951
      finding: Botvinnik annotated his own games himself and describes his system of training in the foreword.

    card title "Find where it turned"

B: Here's a game I lost last week. I'm White.
    draw position

A: It opened quietly. Both sides developed and castled.
    animate plies_shown 12 duration 3.5 easing linear

B: Then I pinned his knight, and he chased my bishop with his pawns.
    animate plies_shown 16 duration 4

A: A few moves later, his knight hit White's queen and bishop.
    animate plies_shown 24 duration 5

    pause 0.6

B: And here's where I lost it. Queen to b4,
    animate plies_shown 25 duration 1.5

B: attacking his bishop and his knight.
    highlight piece_c5 piece_a5

A: And his bishop takes the queen.
    animate plies_shown 26 duration 1.5

B: So the lesson is: don't hang your queen.
    highlight piece_b4

    pause 0.8

A: True, but that was the last mistake, not the first. That pawn on h4 took White's bishop four moves earlier.
    highlight piece_h4

A: To find where a game turned, walk back to the first move that made your position worse.
    animate plies_shown 16 duration 3

B: Move nine. He'd pushed his pawn to g5,
    highlight piece_g5

B: and I played knight to d5.
    animate plies_shown 17 duration 1.5

A: Swap chairs: you're Black now, and the board turns round.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: White's knight just landed on d5. What do you play?
    highlight piece_d5

    pause 0.8

    ask "Punish White's last move." widget chess line true hints ["Captures first.","Your g-pawn."] themes ["hanging piece","threat"] voice a teaches ["safety/hanging-pieces","safety/threats"]
        right: Pawn takes h4: the bishop was attacked, and White moved something else.
        wrong: Which white piece is attacked right now, and by what?

B: The pawn just takes my bishop. I never saw it.
    highlight piece_h4

A: Back to White's side. Before move nine, the game was level.
    animate position_shown 0 plies_shown 16 duration 0.4

A: His pawn attacked the bishop, and White never asked what that move did.
    draw arrow_0

A: Bishop back to g3 keeps the game level.
    draw arrow_1

A: Taking on g5 instead gives a piece for two pawns.
    point at.ref piece_g5 gesture circle

    pause 0.8

    erase arrow_0 arrow_1

A: So the game turned on move nine. By the queen move, White was already a piece down.
    animate plies_shown 24 duration 3.5

    erase position

B: So what do I write down?
    text list_head "Your list" x 500 y 570 font_size 68

A: One line per game: the move, and which of the Three Questions you skipped.
    text item_1 "missed threat" x 500 y 480 font_size 56

A: Write what you were thinking before you switch the engine on. The engine shows the swing; only you know why.
    highlight item_1 effect underline

A: The world champion Mikhail Botvinnik went further: he published his notes, so others could find his mistakes.
    highlight list_head effect underline

    pause 0.5

B: And after a few games?
    highlight item_1

A: Then the list shows you whether the same mistake keeps coming back.
    text item_2 "missed threat" x 500 y 405 font_size 56
    text item_3 "hung piece" x 500 y 330 font_size 56
    text item_4 "missed threat" x 500 y 255 font_size 56
    text item_5 "missed threat" x 500 y 180 font_size 56

A: Here, four missed threats and one hung piece. What do you train first?
    highlight item_1 item_2 item_3 item_4 item_5

    quiz "Which habit do you train first? Pick one." on_canvas true voice a
        right: The missed threat: four lines of five say it, so that habit is the one to drill.
        wrong: Which line keeps coming back?
        * A new opening
        + His threats
        * Endgames

A: Your list is a training plan written by your own games.
    highlight item_1 item_2 item_4 item_5

A: Drill the habit it names, not a new opening.
    highlight list_head effect underline

## Your turn: find the turn
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: All of them. Your list may be shorter than you think.
    good: Most of them. Write the misses down.
    poor: These are exactly the moves that end up on lists. Try them again.
    none: The puzzles will wait for you.
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1bq1rk1/ppp1bppp/2np1n2/8/2BNP3/2N5/PPP1QPPP/R1B2RK1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Nxd4], flip: true}
      - {fen: r4rk1/pbp3pp/8/8/7q/2P5/PP3PPP/R2Q1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qb3+, Kh8, Qxb7]}
      - {fen: r1bq1rk1/ppp1bppp/5n2/4p3/8/2PB1N2/PP3PPP/RN1Q1RK1 b - - 0 1, moves: [e4], flip: true}
      - {fen: 3r2k1/ppq2ppp/8/8/8/8/PP1Q1PPP/3R2K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qxd8+, Qxd8, Rxd8#]}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4, Bc5, c3, Nf6, d4, exd4, cxd4, Bb4+, Bd2, Bxd2+, Nbxd2, d6, O-O, O-O, Re1, Be6, Bxe6, fxe6, Qb3, Qd7, Qxe6+, Qxe6]}
      - {fen: r2q1rk1/ppp2ppp/2npbn2/8/2BPP3/5N2/PP1N1PPP/R2QR1K1 w - - 4 11, moves: [d5], arrows: [{from: d5, to: c6}, {from: d5, to: e6}]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now that you know how to find a turning point: your turn.
    draw position

A: Four puzzles first, each a move club players miss.
    highlight piece_d4

A: You're Black, and White's queen has just stepped off the d-file.
    highlight piece_e2

    pause 0.8

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Captures first.","Who guards the knight on d4?"] themes ["hanging piece"] voice a teaches ["safety/hanging-pieces"]
        right: Knight takes d4: the queen left, and nothing else guarded it.
        wrong: Before anything else, look at every capture you have.

B: Nothing guarded it. He moved his queen and never looked back.
    highlight piece_e2

    pause 0.8

A: Next, White, and you're behind. Checks first.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.8

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Your queen can give check.","Which black piece has no guard?"] themes ["fork","hanging piece"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork","safety/hanging-pieces"]
        right: Queen to b3, check: the king steps aside, and the loose bishop on b7 falls.
        wrong: Try every queen check, and see what else it hits.

    pause 0.8

A: Black again.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.8

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["One pawn, two targets.","Your e-pawn."] themes ["fork"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork"]
        right: Pawn to e4 hits the bishop and the knight. One of them falls.
        wrong: Which pawn move attacks two white pieces at once?

    pause 0.8

A: Last one: calculate to the end.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.8

    ask "Mate in two." widget chess line true hints ["Start with a capture that checks.","After the recapture: your rook on d1."] themes ["back rank","combination"] voice a teaches ["safety/back-rank","calculation/combination"]
        right: Queen takes d8, check; queen takes back; rook takes d8: mate on the back rank.
        wrong: Follow every check to the end, even the one that gives up your queen.

    pause 0.8

B: Now another game of mine. I lost this one too.
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    animate plies_shown 20 duration 5 easing linear

A: Move eleven. His bishop has just come to e6. What would you play?
    animate position_shown 5 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.8

    ask "Your move." widget chess line true hints ["Look at what each pawn can hit.","Your d-pawn."] themes ["fork","threat"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork","safety/threats"]
        right: Pawn to d5 hits the knight and the bishop at once: one of them falls.
        wrong: Which of your pawns can attack two black pieces?

    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 20 duration 0.4

B: I didn't see it. I traded bishops instead.
    animate plies_shown 22 duration 2.4

B: Then queen to b3,
    animate plies_shown 23 duration 1.5

B: and on move thirteen, queen takes e6, check.
    animate plies_shown 25 duration 3

A: And his queen takes back.
    animate plies_shown 26 duration 1.5

    pause 0.8

A: Two mistakes. Which move turned the game: the first move that made White's position worse?
    highlight piece_e6

    fade position to 0.25 duration 0.6

    quiz "Where did this game turn? Pick the move." on_canvas true voice a
        right: Move eleven: pawn to d5 won a piece, and the trade threw that win away. Move thirteen lost the game, but the win was already gone.
        wrong: Find the first move that made White's position worse.
        + Move 11: Bxe6
        * Move 13: Qxe6+
        * Move 10: …Be6

    fade position to 1 duration 0.4

B: So my first mistake was a move I didn't play.
    animate position_shown 5 plies_shown 1 duration 0.6

A: Move eleven threw away a win; move thirteen lost the game.
    draw arrow_0 arrow_1 parallel true

A: Learning from a game means finding its first wrong turn, not its last,
    highlight piece_d5

A: and naming the habit behind it.
    highlight piece_d5

    erase position arrow_0 arrow_1

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower.
    text hw_game "one slow game" x 500 y 520 font_size 52
    text hw_time "15 + 10" x 500 y 430 font_size 52

A: Then find its first mistake, and start your list.
    text hw_mistake "its first mistake" x 500 y 340 font_size 52

## Where next
end_page: true

    text end_next_kicker "Next" x 500 y 540 font_size 26 style.opacity 0.6
    link end_next href lecture:7 title "Week 1 review: habits under pressure" form title size 600 x 500 y 420

    link end_again href lecture:6 title "Watch again" form text mark replay open here x 500 y 250 font_size 28
    text end_count "6 of 30" x 500 y 180 font_size 22 style.opacity 0.6
---
